{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524031719,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE1ODA2NDYzNTI="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1524031719,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE1ODA2NDYzNTM="},"added":["feedback-desired"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1524595969,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4NDA0MTMyMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2428#issuecomment-384041322"},"message":"After initial though I was about to agree without arguing, but then did `git grep 'add.*save.*False'` and got some points possibly to think about/discuss:\n\n- all our `dataset` methods add semantic/functionality for dealing with the paths etc, so at times cannot be just replaced with native git/annex invocations that easily\n- we do, (although just in very few non-tests places) in the code, and I remember doing myself, stage multiple things to be committed with `add --no-save`, so I do still feel that having such functionality could be useful since we do not have some kind of batch processing etc\n- if `add` to become `save`, `save(save=False)` it would look awkward...  well, we could make it `save(add_only=True)` to cover those few cases\n\n- `add PATH` and `add -d DSPATH PATH` have different (and often consuming) semantic already whenever PATH points to a git repo which is not yet a submodule.  I wonder if `save` then would inherit it (if it doesn't yet)?\n- long versions of `-S` should be unified (` --ds2super, --datasets-to-super` in `add` and `--super-datasets` in `save`) but I am not sure even if they mean the same thing","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1526114443,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4ODU0MDIyNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2428#issuecomment-388540224"},"message":"\u003e we do, (although just in very few non-tests places) in the code, and I remember doing myself, stage multiple things to be committed with add --no-save, so I do still feel that having such functionality could be useful since we do not have some kind of batch processing etc\n\nQ ist whether we do that, because `save` wasn't supposed to touch files that aren't known to git in some way. So this would have to be looked at.\n\n\u003e add PATH and add -d DSPATH PATH have different (and often consuming) semantic already whenever PATH points to a git repo which is not yet a submodule. I wonder if save then would inherit it (if it doesn't yet)?\n\nI am trying to guess what you mean here: you like that it \"adds\" all the way up to the reference dataset. If that is correct, then yes, `save` should do that too, and already does AFAIK.\n\n\u003e long versions of -S should be unified (--ds2super, --datasets-to-super in add and --super-datasets in save) but I am not sure even if they mean the same thing\n\nWe better look into `-d^` again for a more flexible solution that would benefit more than just this command, but wasn't cooked either (e.g. #1024)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528435614,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5NTY1MTUwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2428#issuecomment-395651505"},"message":"If we need more arguments to merge the two: V6 is one https://github.com/datalad/datalad/pull/2630/commits/34874176c1845825b4b86ba4a082678bb8f0ae09","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1528798877,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5NjU0MTA2OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2428#issuecomment-396541068"},"message":"Also ping: #1316","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274